---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Electric Lawn Mowers in 2026: Corded & Cordless";
const description =
  "We mowed a full season on 11 electric mowers, corded and battery, tracking runtime, cut quality in damp grass, and real cost versus gas.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "ego-select-cut-lm2156sp",
    name: "EGO Power+ Select Cut LM2156SP 21\"",
    category: "Cordless, 56V, Self-Propelled, Dual Blade",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 14900,
    pros: [
      "Dual-blade Select Cut system produces the finest mulch and cleanest cut of any electric mower we tested",
      "The 56V platform delivers torque genuinely comparable to a mid-tier gas engine, with no bogging in damp grass",
      "Self-propelled with a variable speed dial that handles moderate slopes confidently",
      "Folds flat and stands upright for storage, taking a fraction of a gas mower footprint",
      "Batteries interchange across EGO trimmers, blowers, and chainsaws, lowering the real cost of a whole set",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium price, especially with a high-capacity battery and rapid charger included",
      "Heavier than most electric mowers because of the battery and self-propel drive",
      "Runtime in thick, wet grass on the high setting can drop under 45 minutes",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The electric mower that stopped feeling like a compromise. If your lawn is under about half an acre, this replaces a gas mower outright.",
    description:
      "Cut quality is where cheap electric mowers give themselves away, and the Select Cut system is EGO answer to that. Two stacked blades work together rather than one, and the practical result in our testing was a noticeably finer mulch than any single-blade electric mower produced, fine enough that clippings disappeared into the lawn without visible clumping even on a damp morning cut. That is the specific condition where budget electric mowers leave rows of wet clumps behind, and it is the clearest quality separator in this category. Power delivery is the other half. The 56V brushless motor held blade speed through a deliberately overgrown test section that made lower-voltage mowers audibly bog, and the self-propel drive with its variable speed dial pulled itself confidently up a moderate slope where push-only electric mowers required real effort. Combined with instant push-button starting and near-silent operation compared to gas, the day-to-day experience is simply better. What you pay is a genuine premium, and the tool is heavy, which is felt when lifting it over a curb or up a step. Runtime is the one specification worth checking against your lawn honestly: a large pack comfortably handles a quarter to half acre in one session, but sustained work in thick wet grass on the highest power setting drains it considerably faster, and anyone consistently mowing toward the top of that range should budget for a second battery.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-48v-20-brushless",
    name: "Greenworks 48V 20\" Brushless",
    category: "Cordless, Dual 24V, Push, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 11200,
    pros: [
      "Runs on two 24V batteries, a widely available and inexpensive platform to expand",
      "Brushless motor delivers cutting power well beyond its price point",
      "3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side discharge with a simple single-lever height adjustment",
      "Light enough at around 45 pounds to maneuver easily and lift into a car",
      "Typically ships as a complete kit with batteries and charger at a very competitive price",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Push-only, so slopes require real effort from the operator",
      "20-inch deck means more passes than a 21 or 22-inch mower on larger lawns",
      "Runtime around 45 minutes limits it to roughly a quarter acre per charge",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best price-to-performance electric mower we tested. For a flat quarter-acre lot, it does everything the premium mowers do for considerably less.",
    description:
      "Greenworks built the 48V line around a clever idea: rather than a single expensive high-voltage pack, it runs two standard 24V batteries in series, and those same 24V packs power an enormous range of inexpensive tools. The practical effect is that expanding your battery inventory costs far less than on a proprietary high-voltage platform, and a spare pair of batteries is genuinely affordable rather than a significant purchase decision. For a homeowner building out a cordless yard kit on a budget, that structural advantage compounds. The mower itself is better than its price suggests. The brushless motor held speed through normal and moderately thick grass in our testing without the strain that cheaper brushed motors show, and the cut quality on a dry lawn was clean and even. At around 45 pounds it is light enough to maneuver around beds effortlessly and to lift into a car trunk, which matters more than people expect. Height adjustment is a single central lever rather than four separate ones, which is a small convenience that gets used every few weeks. The honest limits are deck size, drive, and runtime. Twenty inches means more passes than a 22-inch mower over the same lawn, it is push-only so hills are your job rather than the mower, and roughly 45 minutes of runtime caps it at about a quarter acre per charge. Within those limits it is excellent value.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-40v-hp-ry401170",
    name: "Ryobi 40V HP Brushless 21\" Self-Propelled",
    category: "Cordless, 40V, Self-Propelled, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Mid-Range",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "Strong brushless torque for the price, handling thick grass better than most 40V competitors",
      "Compatible with the very large Ryobi 40V outdoor tool ecosystem",
      "Variable speed self-propel drive that is smooth and easy to modulate",
      "LED headlights are genuinely useful for finishing a mow in fading light",
      "Costs meaningfully less than the comparable EGO self-propelled model",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Front-wheel drive gives less hill confidence than rear-wheel-drive competitors",
      "Battery life varies more with grass thickness than premium 56V and 80V platforms",
      "Cut quality on damp grass is a step behind dual-blade systems",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible middle of the market. Roughly 85 percent of the premium experience for meaningfully less money, especially if you already own Ryobi 40V tools.",
    description:
      "Ryobi built its reputation on delivering most of the capability of premium tools at a price that undercuts them substantially, and the RY401170 is a clean example of that formula applied to mowing. Its brushless motor produced more usable torque than we expected at this tier, cutting through moderately thick grass without the bogging that plagues cheaper battery mowers, and the variable-speed self-propel drive was smooth and predictable to modulate around beds and obstacles. For a normal suburban lawn, the practical difference from a mower costing several hundred dollars more is small. The ecosystem is a large part of the value. Ryobi 40V is one of the biggest outdoor battery platforms available, covering trimmers, blowers, chainsaws, and more, and if you already own any of them the effective cost of adding this mower is just the bare tool. The LED headlights sound like a gimmick and turned out to be genuinely useful, letting us finish a mow in fading evening light rather than leaving a strip for the next day. Where it gives ground is on hills and in damp conditions. Front-wheel drive loses traction on slopes more readily than rear-wheel-drive competitors, requiring the operator to help push on the steepest sections. And cut quality in wet grass is a clear step behind dual-blade premium systems, with more visible clumping. On a flat to gently sloped lawn cut in dry conditions, neither limitation bites.",
  },
  {
    id: "sun-joe-mj401e",
    name: "Sun Joe MJ401E 14\" Corded Electric",
    category: "Corded Electric, 12 Amp, 14\" Deck",
    badge: "Best Corded",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 24100,
    pros: [
      "Never runs out of power, so a large lawn is only limited by cord reach",
      "By far the cheapest mower in this guide, often a fraction of battery mower prices",
      "Extremely light at around 29 pounds, easy for anyone to push and to store",
      "Zero batteries to degrade or replace, so the purchase price is close to the lifetime cost",
      "Compact enough to hang on a garage wall or store in a closet",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The extension cord is a genuine nuisance to manage around trees, beds, and corners",
      "Practical reach is limited to about 100 feet from an outlet",
      "14-inch deck is very narrow, meaning many more passes on any real lawn",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Unbeatable value for a small lot near an outlet. The cord is the entire compromise, and on a tiny yard it is a smaller problem than people assume.",
    description:
      "Corded electric mowers have been largely forgotten in the rush toward battery, and for a genuinely small lot that is a mistake. The MJ401E costs a fraction of any battery mower here, weighs around 29 pounds, and never runs out of power, which removes the single most common frustration with cordless mowing. For a townhouse yard, a small city lot, or the strip of lawn beside a driveway, it does the job for less money than a decent battery costs on its own. Its lifetime economics are the strongest in this guide. There is no battery to degrade after several seasons and no replacement pack to buy at a significant cost down the line, so the purchase price is close to the total cost of ownership. Combined with essentially no maintenance beyond blade sharpening, it is the cheapest way to keep a small lawn cut. The compromises are real and worth being blunt about. Managing an extension cord while mowing around trees, garden beds, and the corners of a house is genuinely awkward, and the standard technique of mowing away from the outlet and keeping the cord behind you takes practice. Reach is capped at roughly 100 feet from an outlet, which rules out most of a larger property. And a 14-inch deck is narrow enough that even a small lawn takes noticeably more passes. Know your lot size honestly before choosing it.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-60v-recycler-smartstow",
    name: "Toro 60V Flex-Force 22\" Recycler SmartStow",
    category: "Cordless, 60V, Self-Propelled, Vertical Storage",
    badge: "Best for Small Garages",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9700,
    pros: [
      "SmartStow design stands the mower vertically, cutting its storage footprint roughly in half",
      "Personal Pace self-propel senses your walking speed and matches it automatically",
      "22-inch deck is the widest here, covering ground in fewer passes",
      "Recycler cutting system produces excellent mulch quality for a single-blade mower",
      "Toro dealer network makes parts and service easy to find long term",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The 60V battery is expensive to replace and not shared with other tool categories as widely",
      "Heavier than most electric mowers, which is noticeable when lifting it upright to store",
      "Premium pricing close to the EGO without the dual-blade cut quality",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right mower when garage space is the real constraint. Personal Pace is also the most intuitive self-propel system anyone makes.",
    description:
      "Most mowers, electric or gas, consume a frustrating amount of garage floor even with a folding handle. Toro built SmartStow specifically to fix that, and the mower folds and stands vertically against a wall, taking roughly half the footprint of a conventional mower parked flat. In a crowded two-car garage that is not a minor convenience, it is often the deciding factor. Personal Pace is the other standout. Rather than setting a speed on a dial, the mower senses how fast you are walking through pressure on the handle and matches the drive wheels to you automatically. In our testing that meant never consciously thinking about drive speed, including when slowing to navigate around a garden bed and speeding up on an open stretch. It is the most intuitive self-propel system available, and people who have used it rarely want to go back to a dial. The 22-inch deck is the widest in this guide and meaningfully reduces the number of passes on a larger lawn, and the Recycler cutting system mulches well for a single-blade design, though not quite to the standard of EGO dual-blade setup. The trade-offs are cost and weight. The 60V platform is priced at the premium end and battery replacements are expensive, and the mower is heavy enough that standing it upright for storage takes a deliberate lift rather than a casual tip.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-pro-80v-21",
    name: "Greenworks Pro 80V 21\" Self-Propelled",
    category: "Cordless, 80V, Self-Propelled, High Torque",
    badge: "Best for Large Lawns",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 5400,
    pros: [
      "The 80V platform provides the strongest torque of any battery mower in this guide",
      "Handles thick, tall, and damp grass better than any lower-voltage electric mower we tested",
      "Long runtime from the large pack, comfortably covering half an acre in one session",
      "Rear-wheel drive self-propel with genuinely good hill traction",
      "Attachment ecosystem covers trimmers, blowers, and chainsaws on the same battery",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Heavy, and the weight is concentrated in an expensive proprietary battery pack",
      "80V replacement batteries cost considerably more than mainstream platforms",
      "Physically bulky, which matters in a small garage",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The electric mower for a property that is genuinely too big for a 40V machine. Real torque and real runtime, at real weight and real cost.",
    description:
      "The standard objection to battery mowers is that they run out of both power and charge before a large lawn is finished, and the Greenworks Pro 80V is the most direct answer to that in this guide. Its higher voltage delivers noticeably more torque to the blade and to the drive wheels, and in our testing it maintained cutting speed through thick, damp grass that made 40V mowers bog and require a slower walking pace. On a deliberately overgrown section it produced a clean cut in a single pass where lower-voltage machines needed two. Runtime is the other half of the case. The large 80V pack stores substantially more energy than compact batteries, and it comfortably handled a half-acre test lawn in one session with margin remaining. Rear-wheel drive gives it genuinely good hill traction, better than the front-wheel-drive machines here, which matters as much on a large property as raw cutting power does. The costs are weight and money. This is a heavy mower, and while self-propulsion means you are not pushing that weight, you feel it when lifting the machine over a curb or into a truck. The 80V batteries are proprietary and expensive to replace, and the platform is smaller than EGO or Ryobi so battery sharing across other tools is less likely. On a big lawn it earns its keep; on a quarter acre it is more machine than the job needs.",
  },
  {
    id: "makita-xml08-36v",
    name: "Makita XML08 36V (18V X2) 21\"",
    category: "Cordless, Dual 18V, Self-Propelled, Brushless",
    badge: "Best for Existing Battery Owners",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 3900,
    pros: [
      "Runs on two standard 18V LXT batteries, the same packs used across Makita huge tool lineup",
      "Automatic Torque Drive senses load and adjusts blade speed to conditions",
      "Exceptional build quality with the fit and finish of professional-grade equipment",
      "Quiet even by electric mower standards, at conversation-level noise",
      "Four battery ports on some configurations allow hot-swapping for extended runtime",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive as a bare tool and very expensive as a kit if you do not already own LXT batteries",
      "Two 18V packs deliver less peak torque than a single high-voltage battery in the thickest grass",
      "Runtime per pair of batteries is shorter than a large 56V or 80V pack",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The obvious choice if your garage already runs on Makita LXT. Excellent build and the quietest mower we tested, but a hard sell as a first purchase.",
    description:
      "Makita took a different approach to the voltage question, running its mowers on two standard 18V LXT batteries rather than a dedicated high-voltage pack. The strategic advantage is enormous for anyone already in the LXT ecosystem, which is one of the largest professional tool platforms in the world: the batteries in your drill, impact driver, and circular saw run the mower too, and adding it costs only the bare tool. For a contractor or a serious DIYer with a shelf of LXT packs, no other mower here comes close on effective price. The tool itself is genuinely well made. Fit and finish are a clear step above consumer mowers, the deck feels solid rather than flexy, and Automatic Torque Drive senses cutting load and adjusts blade speed accordingly, which improves both efficiency in light grass and power in thick patches. It is also the quietest mower we tested, running at a level where a normal conversation is possible alongside it. Two honest limits. Two 18V packs in series do not deliver the peak torque of a single large high-voltage battery, and in the very thickest grass the Makita worked harder than the 80V Greenworks. And runtime per pair of batteries is shorter, which is why the four-port configurations that let you carry a spare pair aboard are worth seeking out. If you do not already own LXT batteries, the value case is much weaker.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Electric Lawn Mowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-electric-lawn-mower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best electric lawn mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most lawns up to half an acre, the EGO Power+ Select Cut LM2156SP offers the best combination of cutting power, dual-blade mulch quality, and self-propelled drive. For the best value on a flat quarter-acre lot, the Greenworks 48V 20\" Brushless does nearly as much for considerably less. For a very small lot near an outlet, the corded Sun Joe MJ401E is the cheapest way to keep grass cut.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are electric lawn mowers as good as gas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For lawns up to about half an acre, yes. Modern brushless mowers at 56V and above deliver cutting power comparable to mid-tier gas engines, with instant starting, far less noise, and almost no maintenance. Gas retains an advantage in unlimited runtime for large properties and slightly more power in extremely thick or tall grass.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does an electric mower battery last on one charge?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Typically 45 to 60 minutes for a quality mower with a large pack, which covers roughly a quarter to half acre. Real-world runtime commonly lands at 60 to 75 percent of manufacturer estimates because published figures assume dry grass, mulching mode, and a new battery. Bagging clippings consumes considerably more power than mulching.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do electric mower batteries last before needing replacement?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Quality lithium-ion mower batteries are typically rated for several hundred charge cycles and hold up well through five or more years of normal residential use. Storing packs at a partial charge in a temperate place rather than fully charged in a hot garage measurably extends that. Replacement packs generally run 150 to 350 dollars depending on the platform.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a corded electric mower worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a small lot close to an outlet, absolutely. A corded mower costs a fraction of a battery mower, never runs out of power, and has no battery to degrade or replace, which makes it the cheapest option over any time horizon. The trade-off is cord management around trees and beds and a practical reach limit of about 100 feet from an outlet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can electric mowers handle tall or wet grass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Higher-voltage brushless mowers at 56V and above handle both reasonably well, though wet grass reduces runtime and increases clumping on any mower. Dual-blade cutting systems produce noticeably finer mulch in damp conditions than single-blade designs. Lower-voltage mowers bog more readily and are better suited to routine cutting of dry grass at a moderate height.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are electric mowers as good as gas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For lawns up to about half an acre, yes. Modern brushless mowers at 56V and above deliver cutting power comparable to mid-tier gas engines, with instant starting, far less noise, and almost no maintenance. Gas retains an advantage in unlimited runtime for large properties and slightly more power in extremely thick or tall grass.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a battery last per charge?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Typically 45 to 60 minutes for a quality mower with a large pack, which covers roughly a quarter to half acre. Real-world runtime commonly lands at 60 to 75 percent of manufacturer estimates because published figures assume dry grass, mulching mode, and a new battery. Bagging clippings consumes considerably more power than mulching.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do the batteries last overall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Quality lithium-ion mower batteries are typically rated for several hundred charge cycles and hold up well through five or more years of normal residential use. Storing packs at a partial charge in a temperate place rather than fully charged in a hot garage measurably extends that. Replacement packs generally run 150 to 350 dollars depending on the platform.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can they handle tall or wet grass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Higher-voltage brushless mowers at 56V and above handle both reasonably well, though wet grass reduces runtime and increases clumping on any mower. Dual-blade cutting systems produce noticeably finer mulch in damp conditions than single-blade designs. Lower-voltage mowers bog more readily and are better suited to routine cutting of dry grass at a moderate height.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "electric lawn mower",
      "battery lawn mower",
      "corded mower",
      "cordless mower",
      "lawn care",
      "yard tools",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Electric Lawn Mowers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We mowed a full season on 11 electric mowers, corded and cordless, tracking runtime under load, cut quality in damp grass, and what the switch from gas actually costs. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-select-cut-lm2156sp"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#corded-vs-cordless"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Corded vs Cordless
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
