        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Electric Mowers Finally Make Sense -->
        <div id="why-electric-now" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Electric Mowers Finally Make Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric mowers were a genuine compromise for a long time. Early cordless models had weak motors, short runtimes, and cut quality that fell apart in anything but short, dry grass. Three specific changes closed most of that gap, and it is worth understanding them because they explain which electric mowers are worth buying now and which still are not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brushless motors.</strong> Replacing carbon brushes with electronic commutation cut energy losses substantially, which means more of the battery capacity reaches the blade instead of becoming waste heat. A brushless motor also holds speed under load far better, which is what prevents the bogging that made older electric mowers stall in thick grass. If a battery mower is not brushless, it belongs to the previous generation regardless of when it was manufactured.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Higher voltage platforms.</strong> Moving from 20V and 40V to 56V, 60V, and 80V raised the torque ceiling enough to match mid-tier gas engines. Voltage is not a perfect proxy for power, but across the mowers we tested it tracked closely with how well a machine handled damp, thick grass without slowing down.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery energy density.</strong> Modern lithium packs store considerably more energy per pound than the packs of a decade ago, which turned 20-minute mowers into 45 to 60-minute mowers. That single change moved the practical ceiling from a small courtyard to roughly half an acre.
          </p>

          <p>
            The result is that for lawns up to about half an acre, an electric mower now does the job as well as gas while starting instantly, running quietly, and requiring almost no maintenance. Above that size, gas still has a runtime argument, and above about an acre a riding mower is usually the right answer regardless of power source.
          </p>

