        <!-- SECTION 2: Corded vs Cordless Electric -->
        <div id="corded-vs-cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Corded vs Cordless Electric
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both are electric mowers, and they suit genuinely different properties. The cord is the entire difference, and it is a bigger factor in one direction and a smaller one in the other than most buyers expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded Electric</h3>

          <p>
            A corded mower is the cheapest way to cut grass, full stop. It never runs out of power, it has no battery to degrade or replace, and it typically weighs half what a battery mower does. The cost of ownership over ten years is dramatically lower than any other option here, because the purchase price is very close to the total price. The limitation is reach and cord management. Practical range is around 100 feet from an outlet, and mowing around trees, beds, and house corners while keeping a cord behind you and out of the blade takes real technique. On a small, open lot it is a minor annoyance. On a lot with obstacles it is a constant one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless Battery</h3>

          <p>
            A cordless mower gives you the freedom of a gas mower with the convenience of electric. No cord to manage, no fuel to mix, instant starting, and quiet operation. What you pay for that is the battery, which is the single most expensive component and the one that eventually degrades. Runtime caps the lawn size you can handle in one session, and a second battery is often a practical necessity above a quarter acre.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If your lawn is small, close to an outlet, and relatively open, a corded mower saves a remarkable amount of money for very little real inconvenience. If your lawn has obstacles, is larger than a couple thousand square feet, or is any distance from power, buy cordless and do not look back. The cord frustration on the wrong property is the fastest route to a mower that never gets used.
          </p>

