        <!-- SECTION 4: Battery Runtime: Doing the Math Before You Buy -->
        <div id="runtime-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Battery Runtime: Doing the Math Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Runtime is the specification most likely to disappoint, largely because manufacturer figures are measured under ideal conditions that do not resemble a real lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Understand what amp-hours mean.</strong> Battery capacity is rated in amp-hours, and total energy is roughly voltage multiplied by amp-hours. A 56V 5.0Ah pack holds substantially more energy than a 40V 4.0Ah pack, which is why comparing amp-hours alone across different voltages is misleading. Compare watt-hours if the manufacturer lists them, or simply compare stated runtime figures from the same manufacturer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Discount manufacturer runtime estimates.</strong> Published figures typically assume dry grass at a moderate cutting height with the mower in mulching mode and a fresh battery. Real conditions, damp grass, a lower cut, bagging rather than mulching, and a battery a few seasons old, routinely produce 60 to 75 percent of the advertised number. Plan around the lower figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Estimate your lawn honestly.</strong> A rough rule is that a quarter acre takes roughly 25 to 35 minutes of actual mowing at a normal pace with a 21-inch deck. Half an acre roughly doubles it. If your realistic mowing time approaches the discounted runtime figure, you need either a larger battery or a second one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A second battery is often better value than a bigger one.</strong> Two moderate packs usually cost less than one very large pack, give you the option to swap mid-mow with no downtime, and provide redundancy when one eventually degrades. On any lawn near the edge of single-charge capacity, this is the more practical purchase.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Setting That Costs You the Most Runtime</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Bagging clippings consumes considerably more battery than mulching, because the mower has to generate the airflow to lift and carry clippings into the bag. Switching to mulching mode where lawn conditions allow it can extend runtime meaningfully on the same battery, and it returns nitrogen to the lawn as a bonus. Cutting height matters too: taking off less material per pass is easier work for the motor.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Cut Quality: What Separates Good Electric Mowers From Cheap Ones -->
        <div id="cut-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cut Quality: What Separates Good Electric Mowers From Cheap Ones
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a dry lawn at a moderate height, almost any electric mower produces an acceptable cut. Differences appear in the harder conditions, and those are the conditions worth testing for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blade Speed Under Load</h3>

          <p>
            A clean cut requires the blade to be moving fast enough to slice grass rather than tear it. When a motor bogs in thick grass, blade tip speed drops and the cut quality degrades visibly, leaving ragged, whitened blade tips that brown within a day. Brushless motors and higher voltage platforms hold speed under load far better, which is the underlying reason they cut better in the conditions that matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck Design and Airflow</h3>

          <p>
            A well-designed deck creates a vacuum that lifts grass blades upright before they are cut, producing a more even cut height and better mulching. Cheap flat decks do this poorly, which is why budget mowers leave a wavy, uneven finish that becomes obvious across a large lawn.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single Blade vs Dual Blade</h3>

          <p>
            Dual-blade systems cut the clippings a second time before they are discharged, producing a much finer mulch that settles into the lawn rather than sitting on top in visible clumps. This difference is most obvious in damp grass, which is exactly where single-blade mowers leave rows of wet clippings behind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blade Sharpness</h3>

          <p>
            The most controllable factor and the most neglected. A dull blade tears grass rather than cutting it, leaving frayed tips that turn brown and give a whole lawn a dull cast within days. Sharpening once or twice a season transforms the cut quality of any mower, and it is the cheapest upgrade available for a mower you already own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Electric vs Gas: The Real Cost Over Five Years -->
        <div id="cost-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Electric vs Gas: The Real Cost Over Five Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticker prices make electric mowers look expensive and gas mowers look cheap. Running the numbers over a realistic ownership period changes the picture considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas ongoing costs.</strong> A typical gas mower running 25 to 30 mowing sessions a season consumes roughly 60 to 100 dollars in fuel annually, plus 30 to 50 dollars a year in oil, spark plugs, and air filters. Add an occasional tune-up, carburetor cleaning, or starter repair every few years and five-year upkeep commonly lands in the 600 to 900 dollar range on top of the purchase price.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Electric ongoing costs.</strong> Electricity to recharge a mower battery costs a few dollars per season at most. There is no oil, no filters, no spark plugs, and no fuel stabilizer. The one real long-term cost is the battery itself, which typically holds up well for five or more years of residential use and then costs somewhere between 150 and 350 dollars to replace depending on the platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The crossover.</strong> A premium electric mower that costs several hundred dollars more up front than a comparable gas mower generally reaches cost parity somewhere in year three or four, and comes out ahead thereafter even accounting for an eventual battery replacement. A corded electric mower is cheaper than everything from day one and stays that way.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The costs that are not financial.</strong> Electric mowers eliminate winter fuel stabilization, spring carburetor problems, pull-starting a cold engine, storing gasoline in the garage, and roughly 20 decibels of noise. Several of those are worth more to most homeowners than the dollar difference, and none of them show up in a price comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintaining an Electric Mower -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintaining an Electric Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric mowers need far less attention than gas, but the little maintenance they do need is concentrated in two places: the blade and the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sharpen the blade at least once a season.</strong> This is the single highest-impact maintenance task on any mower and it is trivially easy. A sharp blade slices cleanly; a dull one tears, leaving frayed brown tips across the lawn. Removing the blade takes a socket wrench and a block of wood to stop it turning, and sharpening takes ten minutes with a file or a bench grinder. Keeping a spare blade means you can swap and sharpen at leisure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the underside of the deck.</strong> Wet clippings pack into the deck and restrict the airflow that lifts grass for cutting, which degrades cut quality and forces the motor to work harder. Remove the battery first, tip the mower on its side per the manual, and scrape the deck out with a plastic putty knife every few mows during heavy growth.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store batteries properly.</strong> Lithium packs last longest stored at a partial charge, roughly half, in a temperate location. Storing a fully charged battery in a hot garage all summer or a freezing one all winter measurably shortens its life. Bringing packs indoors for winter is the single best thing you can do for their longevity.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not spray water into the deck.</strong> Electric mowers have motors and electronics in places gas mowers do not, and hosing out the deck can force water into bearings and control boards. Scrape rather than spray, and if you must rinse, keep water away from the motor housing, battery bay, and controls.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the height adjustment mechanism.</strong> Grass and grit accumulate in height adjusters and make them stiff or inaccurate, leading to uneven cutting heights across the deck. Wiping them clean occasionally keeps all four wheels at genuinely the same height, which matters more for cut appearance than most people realize.
          </p>
        </div>

