              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric mowers as good as gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For lawns up to about half an acre, yes. Modern brushless mowers at 56V and above deliver cutting power comparable to mid-tier gas engines, with instant starting, far less noise, and almost no maintenance. Gas retains an advantage in unlimited runtime for large properties and slightly more power in extremely thick or tall grass.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a battery last per charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 45 to 60 minutes for a quality mower with a large pack, which covers roughly a quarter to half acre. Real-world runtime commonly lands at 60 to 75 percent of manufacturer estimates because published figures assume dry grass, mulching mode, and a new battery. Bagging clippings consumes considerably more power than mulching.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do the batteries last overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quality lithium-ion mower batteries are typically rated for several hundred charge cycles and hold up well through five or more years of normal residential use. Storing packs at a partial charge in a temperate place rather than fully charged in a hot garage measurably extends that. Replacement packs generally run 150 to 350 dollars depending on the platform.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a corded electric mower worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a small lot close to an outlet, absolutely. A corded mower costs a fraction of a battery mower, never runs out of power, and has no battery to degrade or replace, which makes it the cheapest option over any time horizon. The trade-off is cord management around trees and beds and a practical reach limit of about 100 feet from an outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can they handle tall or wet grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Higher-voltage brushless mowers at 56V and above handle both reasonably well, though wet grass reduces runtime and increases clumping on any mower. Dual-blade cutting systems produce noticeably finer mulch in damp conditions than single-blade designs. Lower-voltage mowers bog more readily and are better suited to routine cutting of dry grass at a moderate height.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Electric Mower Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric mower disappointment follows a very predictable pattern, and it is nearly always about matching the machine to the lawn.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Too Little Battery for the Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mower rated for 45 minutes is genuinely fine for a quarter acre and will leave you stranded halfway through a half-acre lawn waiting on a charger. Measure your lawn, estimate roughly 30 minutes per quarter acre with a 21-inch deck, discount the manufacturer runtime figure by about a third, and buy accordingly or plan on a second pack.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Corded for a Lot Full of Obstacles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Corded mowers are excellent value on a small open lot and genuinely miserable on a lot with mature trees, garden beds, and a house to work around. The cord snags, doubles back, and has to be repositioned constantly. Be honest about your lot layout rather than only its size before choosing corded.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull blade undoes everything a good mower does, tearing grass instead of cutting it and leaving a whole lawn looking brown-tipped and dull within days. Sharpening takes ten minutes and costs nothing, and it improves cut quality more than upgrading to a more expensive mower would.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Batteries Fully Charged in a Hot Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heat and a full state of charge are the two things that degrade lithium batteries fastest, and a garage in summer combines both. Store packs at roughly half charge somewhere temperate, and bring them indoors for winter. This one habit adds seasons to the life of the most expensive component on the mower.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

